.TH PTY_SEND 3 2026-10-03 "libptytest 0.1" "libptytest Manual"
.SH NAME
pty_send, pty_send_str, pty_send_key \- type into the program under test
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ptytest.h>
.PP
.BI "int pty_send(PtySession *" s ", const char *" bytes ", size_t " len );
.BI "int pty_send_str(PtySession *" s ", const char *" str );
.BI "int pty_send_key(PtySession *" s ", PtyKey " key );
.PP
.BI "#define PTY_KEY_CTRL(" c ")"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions write to the master side of the PTY: the bytes arrive at
the child's standard input, after the line discipline (so in canonical
mode the tty echoes them and \fBCtrl\-C\fP raises
.BR SIGINT ).
.PP
.BR pty_send ()
writes
.I len
bytes.
When the input queue is full, output is read and fed to the emulator while
waiting for room, so a child that is itself blocked writing cannot
deadlock the test.
.PP
.BR pty_send_str ()
writes a NUL-terminated string.
.PP
.BR pty_send_key ()
writes one key as xterm encodes it:
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_UP, _DOWN, _RIGHT, _LEFT
CSI A..D, or SS3 A..D (see below)
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_HOME, PTY_KEY_END
CSI H / CSI F, or SS3 H / SS3 F
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_PGUP, PTY_KEY_PGDN
CSI 5 ~, CSI 6 ~
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_INSERT, PTY_KEY_DELETE
CSI 2 ~, CSI 3 ~
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_SHIFT_TAB
CSI Z
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_F1 .. PTY_KEY_F4
SS3 P .. SS3 S
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_F5 .. PTY_KEY_F12
CSI 15 ~ .. CSI 24 ~
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_ENTER
CR (0x0d)
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_TAB, PTY_KEY_BACK, PTY_KEY_ESC
0x09, 0x7f, 0x1b
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_CTRL_C, PTY_KEY_CTRL_D
0x03, 0x04
.TP
.B PTY_KEY_CTRL(c)
c & 0x1f
.PP
The cursor keys and Home/End follow the program's DECCKM mode, as a real
terminal does: after the program sends
.B CSI ? 1 h
(every curses program in keypad mode does) they are sent in the SS3 form,
which is the only form such a program recognises.
Any other value from 0 to 255 is sent as that single byte.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 when everything was written; \-1 if there is no running child, the
arguments are invalid, the key code is unknown, writing failed, or the
input could not be written within 5 seconds.
.SH NOTES
Sending is not waiting.
After a key that changes the screen, wait for the change with
.BR pty_wait_for (3)
before inspecting it.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
pty_send_str(s, "hello");
pty_send_key(s, PTY_KEY_ENTER);
pty_send_key(s, PTY_KEY_CTRL('x'));
ASSERT_WAIT_FOR(s, "Saved", 3000);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pty_wait_for (3),
.BR libptytest (7)
